return [
    1 => [
        'title' => 'Major Depressive Disorder',
        'slug' => 'major-depressive-disorder-criteria',
        'description' => 'The 2-week duration threshold, 5 of 9 core symptoms (SIGECAPS), mandatory depressed mood/anhedonia, and functional impairment.',
        'overview' => "Major Depressive Disorder (MDD) is a prevalent, highly disabling mood disorder characterized by discrete episodes of persistent depressed mood, profound loss of interest or pleasure (anhedonia), and a constellation of vegetative, cognitive, and psychomotor disturbances. Far from representing ordinary sadness or transient demoralization, MDD involves profound neurobiological disruption across frontolimbic emotional processing circuits, dysregulation of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis, and reductions in neurotrophic signaling factors such as brain-derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF).\n\nIn DSM-5, Depressive Disorders were established as an independent diagnostic chapter, distinct from Bipolar and Related Disorders. This structural separation emphasizes that unipolar depression follows a fundamentally different longitudinal course, genetic profile, and pharmacological treatment paradigm than bipolar illness. A critical diagnostic principle in MDD is the lifetime exclusion of mania or hypomania: if an individual with recurrent major depressive episodes experiences even a single past manic or hypomanic episode, the diagnosis must be immediately reclassified to Bipolar I or Bipolar II Disorder.\n\nThe clinical presentation of MDD encompasses both emotional suffering and objective physiological alterations. Sleep architecture is characteristically disturbed, showing diminished slow-wave sleep and shortened rapid eye movement (REM) latency. Cognitive slowing, executive dysfunction, and feelings of worthlessness further compound personal distress and occupational impairment.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A requires five (or more) of the following nine symptoms present during the same 2-week period, representing a change from previous functioning, with at least one symptom being either (1) depressed mood or (2) loss of interest or pleasure (anhedonia):\n\n1. Depressed mood most of the day, nearly every day (indicated by subjective report or observation by others; in children/adolescents, can be irritable mood).\n2. Markedly diminished interest or pleasure in all, or almost all, activities most of the day, nearly every day.\n3. Significant weight loss when not dieting or weight gain (e.g., change of >5% body weight in a month), or decrease/increase in appetite nearly every day.\n4. Insomnia or hypersomnia nearly every day.\n5. Psychomotor agitation or retardation nearly every day (observable by others, not merely subjective feelings).\n6. Fatigue or loss of energy nearly every day.\n7. Feelings of worthlessness or excessive or inappropriate guilt nearly every day (which may be delusional, not merely self-reproach).\n8. Diminished ability to think or concentrate, or indecisiveness, nearly every day.\n9. Recurrent thoughts of death (not just fear of dying), recurrent suicidal ideation without a specific plan, or a suicide attempt or specific plan.\n\nCriterion B requires clinically significant distress or impairment in social, occupational, or other important areas. Criterion C mandates the episode is not attributable to physiological effects of a substance or another medical condition. Criterion D requires the occurrence is not better explained by schizoaffective disorder or psychotic disorders. Criterion E establishes that there has NEVER been a manic episode or a hypomanic episode.",
        'specifiers' => "Severity and course specifiers: Single episode vs. Recurrent episode; Mild, Moderate, Severe; With psychotic features (Mood-congruent vs. Mood-incongruent); In partial remission; In full remission.\n\nClinical subtype specifiers: 'With anxious distress', 'With mixed features', 'With melancholic features', 'With atypical features', 'With psychotic features', 'With catatonia', 'With peripartum onset', and 'With seasonal pattern' (seasonal affective disorder).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "MDD vs. Bipolar Disorders: A single lifetime hypomanic episode (>= 4 days) establishes Bipolar II; a single lifetime manic episode establishes Bipolar I. Antidepressant monotherapy in bipolar depression risks manic switching.\n\nMDD vs. Persistent Depressive Disorder (Dysthymia): Dysthymia requires a 2-year duration of depressed mood; if criteria for a major depressive episode are met during dysthymia, both are coded ('double depression').\n\nMDD vs. Medical Conditions: Hypothyroidism, pancreatic carcinoma, anemia, vitamin B12 deficiency, and Parkinson disease can produce vegetative depressive symptoms; thyroid and metabolic labs are standard diagnostic evaluations.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 36-year-old accountant presenting with severe fatigue, difficulty making work decisions, and a 12-pound unintentional weight loss over the past three weeks. The accountant reports feeling completely empty and joyless, unable to enjoy spending time with their children (anhedonia), waking up at 3:00 AM unable to return to sleep (terminal insomnia), and experiencing intrusive thoughts that their family would be better off if they were dead.\n\nMedical workup is unremarkable. The patient exhibits 7 of the 9 DSM-5 criteria (anhedonia, depressed mood, weight loss, insomnia, fatigue, indecisiveness, suicidal ideation) persisting for three weeks. There is no lifetime history of hypomania or mania. The clinical formulation confirms Major Depressive Disorder, Single Episode, Severe.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: MDD requires at least 5 of 9 symptoms for at least 2 weeks, with at least one symptom being depressed mood or anhedonia (SIGECAPS mnemonic: Sleep, Interest, Guilt, Energy, Concentration, Appetite, Psychomotor, Suicidal ideation). Irritable mood can substitute for depressed mood in children and adolescents. Lifetime exclusion of mania and hypomania is absolute. DSM-5 eliminated the bereavement exclusion; bereavement-related depression can be diagnosed if full criteria are met.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'The 2-Week Threshold', 'icon' => '⏱️', 'definition' => 'Symptoms must be present during the same 2-week period and represent an unequivocal change from prior functioning.'],
            ['title' => 'Mandatory Core Cardinal Symptoms', 'icon' => '🎯', 'definition' => 'Diagnosis strictly requires either depressed mood or loss of interest/pleasure (anhedonia) among the 5 symptoms.'],
            ['title' => 'The SIGECAPS Mnemonic', 'icon' => '🧠', 'definition' => 'High-yield clinical framework: Sleep, Interest, Guilt, Energy, Concentration, Appetite, Psychomotor, Suicidal ideation.'],
            ['title' => 'Absolute Bipolar Exclusion', 'icon' => '🚫', 'definition' => 'A lifetime occurrence of a single manic or hypomanic episode permanently excludes unipolar MDD.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
