    2 => [
        'title' => 'Persistent Depressive Disorder (Dysthymia: 2-Year Continuum)',
        'slug' => 'persistent-depressive-disorder-dysthymia',
        'description' => 'The 2-year adult duration continuum (1 year in children), subthreshold and syndromal chronicity, and double depression.',
        'overview' => "Persistent Depressive Disorder (PDD) represents a consolidation in DSM-5 that unified DSM-IV Chronic Major Depressive Disorder and Dysthymic Disorder into a single overarching diagnosis. This integration was driven by extensive longitudinal outcome studies showing that chronic unipolar depressions share common biological vulnerabilities, high familial aggregation, and poor functional prognosis regardless of whether symptoms cross the full syndromal threshold of a major depressive episode.\n\nThe hallmark diagnostic requirement of PDD is its relentless chronicity: an individual must experience depressed mood for most of the day, for more days than not, for at least two continuous years in adults (or at least one year in children and adolescents). During this multi-year period, symptoms must be present for at least half the time, and the individual must not have been symptom-free for more than two consecutive months.\n\nPDD captures diverse clinical presentations, ranging from pure dysthymic syndrome (chronic low-grade depressive symptoms) to persistent major depressive episodes that fail to remit over years. It frequently leads to severe functional impairment that exceeds that of episodic MDD due to its insidious, enduring nature, which leads many patients to perceive their chronic despair as a permanent facet of their personality ('I've always been this way').",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A requires depressed mood for most of the day, for more days than not, as indicated by either subjective account or observation by others, for at least 2 years (in children and adolescents, mood can be irritable and duration must be at least 1 year).\n\nCriterion B mandates the presence, while depressed, of two (or more) of the following six symptoms: (1) Poor appetite or overeating; (2) Insomnia or hypersomnia; (3) Low energy or fatigue; (4) Low self-esteem; (5) Poor concentration or difficulty making decisions; (6) Feelings of hopelessness.\n\nCriterion C establishes the chronicity ceiling: During the 2-year period (1 year in children/adolescents) of the disturbance, the individual has never been without the symptoms in Criteria A and B for more than 2 months at a time.\n\nCriterion D allows that criteria for a major depressive episode may be continuously present for 2 years.\n\nCriterion E mandates that there has NEVER been a manic episode or a hypomanic episode, and criteria have never been met for cyclothymic disorder. Criterion F requires the disturbance is not better explained by a psychotic disorder. Criterion G and H require ruling out substances, medical conditions, and verifying clinical distress or functional impairment.",
        'specifiers' => "Specifiers for PDD indicate onset age: 'Early onset' (onset before age 21 years) or 'Late onset' (onset at age 21 years or older). Early onset is strongly associated with higher personality disorder comorbidity and substance use.\n\nSpecifiers describing current symptom pattern over the most recent 2-year period: (1) 'With pure dysthymic syndrome' (full criteria for a major depressive episode have NOT been met in at least the preceding 2 years); (2) 'With persistent major depressive episode' (full criteria for a major depressive episode have been met throughout the preceding 2-year period); (3) 'With intermittent major depressive episodes, with current episode'; (4) 'With intermittent major depressive episodes, without current episode'.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "PDD vs. Major Depressive Disorder (Episodic): Episodic MDD features discrete episodes with periods of normal euthymia between episodes; PDD features chronic depression persisting for >= 2 years without more than 2 symptom-free months.\n\nDouble Depression: A clinical scenario where a patient with baseline chronic low-grade dysthymic symptoms develops a superimposed full Major Depressive Episode. Under DSM-5, this is coded as PDD with intermittent major depressive episodes, with current episode.\n\nPDD vs. Bipolar Disorders: If hypomanic or manic episodes have ever occurred, PDD is excluded in favor of Bipolar I or Bipolar II Disorder.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 28-year-old librarian who reports having felt 'chronically down and inadequate' since graduating college four years ago. The librarian reports low energy, difficulty concentrating, chronic self-doubt, and poor appetite.\n\nOver the past four years, the librarian has never had more than three or four consecutive weeks where they felt happy or energetic. They have never had suicidal ideation, psychomotor retardation, or anhedonia severe enough to meet full criteria for a major depressive episode. The presence of chronic depressed mood for >2 years with low self-esteem, fatigue, and poor appetite, without symptom-free periods exceeding 2 months, confirms Persistent Depressive Disorder (Dysthymia), with pure dysthymic syndrome, Early onset.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: PDD requires depressed mood for >= 2 years in adults (>= 1 year in children/adolescents), with no more than 2 consecutive months symptom-free. Criterion B requires only 2 of 6 symptoms (appetite changes, sleep changes, fatigue, low self-esteem, poor concentration, hopelessness). Notice that feelings of worthlessness and suicidal ideation are NOT in Criterion B of PDD (they are in MDD). Early onset is defined as onset before age 21.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => '2-Year Adult Chronicity Floor', 'icon' => '🗓️', 'definition' => 'Requires persistent depressed mood for at least 2 continuous years in adults (1 year in pediatric patients).'],
            ['title' => 'The 2-Month Symptom-Free Ceiling', 'icon' => '⏱️', 'definition' => 'During the 2-year period, euthymic intervals cannot exceed 2 consecutive months.'],
            ['title' => '2 of 6 Symptom Menu', 'icon' => '🔢', 'definition' => 'Requires only 2 of 6 symptoms: appetite, sleep, fatigue, low self-esteem, poor concentration, and hopelessness.'],
            ['title' => 'Double Depression Concept', 'icon' => '⚡', 'definition' => 'A full Major Depressive Episode superimposed upon underlying baseline Persistent Depressive Disorder.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Dysthymia', 'definition' => 'A chronic, low-grade depressive state persisting over multiple years, formally categorized under Persistent Depressive Disorder.'],
            ['term' => 'Hopelessness', 'definition' => 'A persistent negative cognitive expectation regarding future outcomes, an explicit Criterion B symptom in PDD that strongly correlates with suicide risk.'],
            ['term' => 'Double Depression', 'definition' => 'A clinical term describing the co-occurrence of a major depressive episode superimposed upon chronic dysthymia.'],
            ['term' => 'Early Onset PDD', 'definition' => 'Persistent Depressive Disorder with onset prior to age 21 years, associated with high chronicity and comorbid personality pathology.']
        ]
    ],
    3 => [
        'title' => 'Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder (DMDD in Children)',
        'slug' => 'disruptive-mood-dysregulation-disorder-dmdd',
        'description' => 'Developmental rage outbursts (>= 3 times/week), chronic inter-outburst irritability, age window (6 to 18 years), and reduction of pediatric bipolar misdiagnosis.',
        'overview' => "Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder (DMDD) was newly added to DSM-5 to address a critical diagnostic crisis: the dramatic, forty-fold over-diagnosis of pediatric bipolar disorder that occurred between the mid-1990s and 2010. During that era, children presenting with severe, chronic, non-episodic irritability and frequent explosive temper outbursts were widely diagnosed with Bipolar Disorder and exposed to complex regimens of antipsychotics and mood stabilizers, despite lacking the distinct, episodic manic cycles characteristic of true bipolar illness.\n\nExtensive longitudinal psychiatric research led by the National Institute of Mental Health (NIMH) established that children with chronic, severe non-episodic irritability do not grow up to develop adult Bipolar I or II Disorder. Instead, their longitudinal developmental trajectory leads overwhelmingly to adult unipolar Major Depressive Disorder and Anxiety Disorders.\n\nTo correct this clinical misattribution, DSM-5 introduced DMDD within the Depressive Disorders chapter. DMDD captures children with frequent severe temper outbursts (at least three times per week) occurring on a background of a persistently irritable or angry mood between outbursts across multiple settings.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A requires severe recurrent temper outbursts manifested verbally (e.g., verbal rages) and/or behaviorally (e.g., physical aggression toward people or property) that are grossly out of proportion in intensity or duration to the situation or provocation.\n\nCriterion B mandates that the temper outbursts are inconsistent with developmental level.\n\nCriterion C requires that the temper outbursts occur, on average, three or more times per week.\n\nCriterion D establishes that the mood between temper outbursts is persistently irritable or angry most of the day, nearly every day, and is observable by others (e.g., parents, teachers, peers).\n\nCriterion E establishes the duration requirement: Criteria A-D have been present for 12 or more months. Throughout that time, the individual has not had a period lasting 3 or more consecutive months without all of the symptoms in Criteria A-D.\n\nCriterion F requires cross-setting manifestation: Symptoms are present in at least two of three settings (at home, at school, with peers) and are severe in at least one of these.\n\nCriterion G establishes strict age boundaries: The diagnosis should NOT be made for the first time before age 6 years or after age 18 years.\n\nCriterion H requires that by history or observation, the age at onset of Criteria A-D is before 10 years.\n\nCriterion I dictates that there has NEVER been a distinct period lasting more than 1 day during which full symptom criteria for a manic or hypomanic episode (except duration) have been met.",
        'specifiers' => "DMDD cannot coexist with Oppositional Defiant Disorder (ODD), Intermittent Explosive Disorder (IED), or Bipolar Disorder. If an individual meets criteria for both DMDD and ODD, only DMDD is diagnosed.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "DMDD vs. Pediatric Bipolar Disorder: Bipolar disorder is episodic, with distinct periods of elevated or expansive mood, grandiosity, and decreased sleep need; DMDD is chronic and non-episodic, with chronic inter-outburst irritability.\n\nDMDD vs. Oppositional Defiant Disorder (ODD): While temper outbursts occur in ODD, the chronic, pervasive inter-outburst irritable mood required in DMDD is absent or mild in ODD. By DSM-5 rule, if criteria for both are met, DMDD takes absolute precedence.\n\nDMDD vs. Autism Spectrum Disorder: In ASD, outbursts frequently occur in response to disruption of routines or sensory overload; DMDD is diagnosed only if outbursts and irritability clearly exceed what is explained by ASD.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves an 8-year-old child referred for extreme behavioral rages. Four to five times per week, the child screams, throws furniture, and hits family members over minor frustrations (e.g., being asked to turn off the television). Between these explosive episodes, the child remains visibly sullen, angry, and easily annoyed both at home and in the classroom.\n\nThis pattern has persisted continuously for 14 months with onset at age 7. The child has never exhibited grandiosity, flight of ideas, or decreased need for sleep. The chronic inter-outburst irritability and frequent temper rages across two settings confirm Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder, ruling out Bipolar Disorder.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: DMDD was introduced specifically to prevent the misdiagnosis of pediatric Bipolar Disorder. Outbursts must occur >= 3 times per week for >= 12 months in at least two settings. The age window: onset must be before age 10; diagnosis cannot be made before age 6 or after age 18. If a child meets criteria for both DMDD and ODD, ONLY DMDD is diagnosed. Longitudinally, children with DMDD are at highest risk for adult unipolar depression and anxiety, NOT bipolar disorder.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Reduction of Pediatric Bipolar Misdiagnosis', 'icon' => '🎯', 'definition' => 'Created to distinguish chronic severe childhood irritability from true cyclical episodic bipolar manic illness.'],
            ['title' => 'Frequency & Duration Rules', 'icon' => '⏱️', 'definition' => 'Outbursts must occur at least 3 times per week, with symptoms persisting continuously for at least 12 months.'],
            ['title' => 'Strict Age Boundaries (6 to 18 Years)', 'icon' => '🧒', 'definition' => 'Cannot be diagnosed before age 6 or after age 18; onset of symptoms must be documented prior to age 10.'],
            ['title' => 'ODD Diagnostic Precedence', 'icon' => '👑', 'definition' => 'If criteria for both ODD and DMDD are satisfied, DMDD takes diagnostic priority and ODD is not coded.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Chronic Irritability', 'definition' => 'A continuous, non-episodic baseline state of anger, touchiness, and annoyance, distinct from episodic affective shifts.'],
            ['term' => 'Developmental Inappropriateness', 'definition' => 'Behavioral outbursts whose intensity, duration, and triggers are markedly aberrant for the child\'s chronological and developmental stage.'],
            ['term' => 'Temper Outburst', 'definition' => 'Severe verbal rages or physical aggression directed against property, animals, or other individuals in response to minor frustration.'],
            ['term' => 'Diagnostic Cannibalization', 'definition' => 'The DSM-5 hierarchy rule wherein DMDD absorbs and overrides the diagnosis of Oppositional Defiant Disorder when both criteria are met.']
        ]
    ],
    4 => [
        'title' => 'Premenstrual Dysphoric Disorder & Substance-Induced Depression',
        'slug' => 'pmdd-substance-induced-depression',
        'description' => 'Luteal phase timing, 5-symptom criterion, prospective daily rating confirmation, and toxicological depressive mimicry.',
        'overview' => "Premenstrual Dysphoric Disorder (PMDD) was elevated from an appendix 'condition for further study' in DSM-IV to a fully validated diagnostic entity in DSM-5. PMDD represents a severe, cyclical mood disorder characterized by affective lability, irritability, depressed mood, and anxiety that emerge specifically during the luteal phase of the menstrual cycle, remit around the onset of menses, and remain completely absent during the postmenses follicular phase.\n\nPMDD is not an endocrine disorder in the traditional sense; circulating peripheral levels of estrogen and progesterone are entirely normal in affected women. Instead, the pathophysiology involves abnormal central nervous system cellular sensitivity to normal cyclical fluctuations in gonadal neurosteroids, particularly allopregnanolone, which alters GABA-A receptor plasticity and serotonergic transmission in frontolimbic circuits.\n\nSubstance/Medication-Induced Depressive Disorder represents a distinct condition where severe depressive symptoms are the direct physiological consequence of a drug of abuse, toxin, or prescription medication. Classic pharmacological triggers include reserpine, beta-blockers, interferon-alpha, systemic corticosteroids, oral contraceptives, and withdrawal from central nervous system stimulants (cocaine, amphetamines).",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "For PMDD: Criterion A requires that in the majority of menstrual cycles, at least 5 symptoms must be present in the final week before the onset of menses, start to improve within a few days after the onset of menses, and become minimal or absent in the week postmenses.\n\nCriterion B mandates at least one (or more) of the following affective symptoms: (1) Marked affective lability (mood swings, sudden sadness/tearfulness, increased rejection sensitivity); (2) Marked irritability or anger or increased interpersonal conflicts; (3) Marked depressed mood, feelings of hopelessness, or self-deprecating thoughts; (4) Marked anxiety, tension, and/or feelings of being keyed up on edge.\n\nCriterion C requires one (or more) of the following to reach a total of 5 symptoms when combined with Criterion B: (1) Decreased interest in usual activities; (2) Subjective difficulty in concentration; (3) Lethargy, easy fatigability, or marked lack of energy; (4) Marked change in appetite, overeating, or specific food cravings; (5) Hypersomnia or insomnia; (6) A sense of being overwhelmed or out of control; (7) Physical symptoms such as breast tenderness/swelling, joint/muscle pain, bloating, or weight gain.\n\nCriterion D requires clinically significant distress or functional interference. Criterion E requires the disturbance is not merely an exacerbation of another disorder. Criterion F mandates prospective daily ratings across at least two symptomatic cycles to confirm the diagnosis.",
        'specifiers' => "For Substance/Medication-Induced Depressive Disorder, clinicians specify the offending substance and indicate: 'With onset during intoxication' or 'With onset during withdrawal'.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "PMDD vs. Premenstrual Syndrome (PMS): PMS is far more common, features predominantly physical symptoms with mild emotional changes, and does not require the 5-symptom threshold or cause severe functional impairment.\n\nPMDD vs. Premenstrual Exacerbation of MDD/GAD: In premenstrual exacerbation, symptoms worsen premenstrually but persist throughout the entire follicular phase; PMDD requires a completely symptom-free follicular interval.\n\nSubstance-Induced Depression vs. Primary MDD: If depressive symptoms persist for > 1 month following complete cessation of substance withdrawal or intoxication, an independent primary MDD is diagnosed.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 31-year-old woman who reports severe depression, rage outbursts, and tearfulness that disrupt her marriage and work every month. She tracks her symptoms prospectively on a daily rating scale for two consecutive months.\n\nThe logs demonstrate that during the 7 days prior to menses, she experiences severe irritability, depressed mood, fatigue, carbohydrate cravings, and breast tenderness (5 symptoms meeting Criteria B and C). Within two days after menstruation begins, her symptoms drop to zero and remain absent for two weeks until ovulation. The prospective confirmation of luteal-phase restriction establishes Premenstrual Dysphoric Disorder.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: PMDD requires at least 5 symptoms during the final week before menses, which remit within a few days of menses onset, confirmed prospectively by daily ratings over at least 2 consecutive menstrual cycles. At least 1 symptom MUST be from Criterion B (mood swings, irritability, depression, anxiety). Circulating hormone levels in PMDD are normal; the disorder reflects central receptor sensitivity. Depressive symptoms persisting >1 month after substance detox indicate primary MDD.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Luteal Phase Cyclical Specificity', 'icon' => '🌙', 'definition' => 'Symptoms manifest strictly during the premenstrual luteal phase and completely remit in the postmenses follicular phase.'],
            ['title' => 'Prospective Daily Rating Mandate', 'icon' => '📅', 'definition' => 'Formal diagnosis requires prospective daily charting over at least two consecutive menstrual cycles to confirm follicular euthymia.'],
            ['title' => 'At Least 1 Affective Symptom', 'icon' => '💔', 'definition' => 'Requires at least one primary emotional symptom: marked affective lability, irritability, depressed mood, or anxiety.'],
            ['title' => 'Allopregnanolone & GABA Neurobiology', 'icon' => '🧠', 'definition' => 'Pathophysiology involves altered central neuronal sensitivity to neurosteroid fluctuations rather than peripheral endocrine hormone abnormalities.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Luteal Phase', 'definition' => 'The second half of the menstrual cycle following ovulation, characterized by the rise and subsequent precipitous fall of progesterone and estrogen.'],
            ['term' => 'Follicular Phase', 'definition' => 'The pre-ovulatory phase of the menstrual cycle, during which individuals with pure PMDD must be completely symptom-free.'],
            ['term' => 'Allopregnanolone', 'definition' => 'A neuroactive metabolite of progesterone that acts as a positive allosteric modulator of GABA-A receptors, central to the neurobiology of PMDD.'],
            ['term' => 'Prospective Charting', 'definition' => 'The real-time daily recording of psychological and somatic symptoms across consecutive menstrual cycles, essential for confirming PMDD.']
        ]
    ],
    5 => [
        'title' => 'Diagnostic Features & Specifiers: Melancholic, Atypical, and Peripartum',
        'slug' => 'specifiers-melancholic-atypical-peripartum-depression',
        'description' => 'Melancholic neurovegetative signs, atypical mood reactivity, leaden paralysis, peripartum timing, and seasonal affective disorder.',
        'overview' => "Clinical specifiers in Depressive Disorders delineate discrete phenotypic subtypes that carry profound neurobiological, prognostic, and therapeutic significance. Rather than representing homogenous entities, major depressive episodes display markedly distinct biological signatures: while one patient experiences profound insomnia, severe weight loss, and non-reactive despair (melancholic depression), another experiences hypersomnia, hyperphagia, and emotional reactivity (atypical depression).\n\nThe 'With melancholic features' specifier identifies a severe biological subtype characterized by pervasive loss of pleasure, lack of mood reactivity, diurnal mood variation (regularly worse in the morning), and early morning terminal awakening. Melancholia shows strong biological markers, including HPA axis hyperactivity (dexamethasone non-suppression) and robust response to somatic treatments (tricyclic antidepressants, ECT) rather than psychotherapy alone.\n\nConversely, the 'With atypical features' specifier is defined by preserved mood reactivity—the capacity for mood to temporarily brighten in response to positive actual or potential events. Accompanied by reversed vegetative signs (hypersomnia, increased appetite) and long-standing interpersonal rejection sensitivity, atypical depression responds preferentially to monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs) and selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs).",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Specifier Formulations under DSM-5:\n\n1. With Melancholic Features: Requires (A) Either loss of pleasure in all activities OR lack of reactivity to usually pleasurable stimuli (does not feel better even temporarily when something good happens), PLUS (B) Three (or more) of the following: (1) Distinct quality of depressed mood (profound despondency, gloom, despair); (2) Depression regularly worse in the morning; (3) Early-morning awakening (at least 2 hours before usual); (4) Marked psychomotor agitation or retardation; (5) Significant anorexia or weight loss; (6) Excessive or inappropriate guilt.\n\n2. With Atypical Features: Requires (A) Mood reactivity (i.e., mood brightens in response to actual or potential positive events), PLUS (B) Two (or more) of the following: (1) Significant weight gain or increase in appetite; (2) Hypersomnia (sleeping >= 10 hours/day or >= 2 hours more than baseline); (3) Leaden paralysis (heavy, leaden feelings in arms or legs); (4) Long-standing pattern of interpersonal rejection sensitivity resulting in significant impairment.\n\n3. With Peripartum Onset: Applied when mood episode onset occurs during pregnancy or in the 4 weeks following delivery.\n\n4. With Seasonal Pattern (Seasonal Affective Disorder): Regular temporal relationship between onset of major depressive episodes and a particular time of the year (e.g., fall/winter) with full remissions in spring, occurring for at least 2 consecutive years without nonseasonal episodes.",
        'specifiers' => "A crucial diagnostic exclusion rule: The specifiers 'With melancholic features' and 'With atypical features' are mutually exclusive and CANNOT be coded concurrently for the same depressive episode because mood reactivity is absent in melancholia and mandatory in atypical depression.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Melancholic vs. Atypical Depression: Assessed primarily via mood reactivity: in melancholia, mood does NOT brighten to positive events; in atypical depression, mood reactivity is mandatory.\n\nPeripartum Depression vs. Baby Blues: Baby blues affects up to 80% of postpartum women, peaks at day 3-5, resolves within 14 days, and causes no marked impairment; peripartum major depression lasts >= 2 weeks and causes severe functional impairment.\n\nSeasonal Affective Disorder vs. Situational Winter Stressors: SAD involves biological vegetative shifts (hypersomnia, carbohydrate craving) responsive to bright light therapy, whereas situational stressors are linked to predictable seasonal unemployment or holidays.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 44-year-old patient presenting with severe depression. When told that their adult child was accepted into medical school, the patient temporarily smiled, felt genuinely happy, and joined a celebratory dinner (mood reactivity present).\n\nHowever, the patient sleeps 11 hours per night (hypersomnia), has gained 15 pounds from excessive carbohydrate eating, reports a heavy 'lead-like' weight in their limbs, and avoids social gatherings due to intense fear of rejection. Meeting the core criterion of mood reactivity alongside hypersomnia, weight gain, leaden paralysis, and rejection sensitivity, the clinical formulation confirms Major Depressive Disorder, With Atypical Features.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Melancholic and Atypical specifiers are mutually exclusive. Melancholia requires non-reactive mood and features terminal insomnia (early morning awakening) and morning worsening. Atypical depression requires mood reactivity and features hypersomnia, hyperphagia, leaden paralysis, and rejection sensitivity. Peripartum onset is restricted to episodes beginning during pregnancy or within 4 weeks postpartum. Seasonal pattern requires 2 consecutive years of seasonal episodes with seasonal remissions.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Mutual Exclusivity Axiom', 'icon' => '↔️', 'definition' => 'Melancholic and Atypical specifiers can never be co-diagnosed because they represent opposite polarities of mood reactivity.'],
            ['title' => 'Melancholic Biological Hallmarks', 'icon' => '🌅', 'definition' => 'Terminal early awakening, diurnal morning worsening, profound unreactive despondency, and severe anorexia.'],
            ['title' => 'Atypical Reversed Vegetative Signs', 'icon' => '😴', 'definition' => 'Mood reactivity paired with hypersomnia, hyperphagia/weight gain, leaden paralysis, and interpersonal rejection sensitivity.'],
            ['title' => 'Light Therapy in Seasonal Depression', 'icon' => '☀️', 'definition' => 'Seasonal affective disorder involves phase-delayed circadian rhythms responsive to 10,000-lux morning bright light exposure.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Mood Reactivity', 'definition' => 'The capacity for an individual\'s depressed mood to temporarily brighten and normalize in response to positive external events.'],
            ['term' => 'Leaden Paralysis', 'definition' => 'A subjective sensation of extreme heaviness, fatigue, or lead-like weight in the arms or legs, characteristic of atypical depression.'],
            ['term' => 'Interpersonal Rejection Sensitivity', 'definition' => 'A long-standing trait wherein an individual experiences extreme emotional pain and functional impairment in response to perceived social rejection.'],
            ['term' => 'Terminal Insomnia', 'definition' => 'Early morning awakening at least two hours before expected waking time with inability to return to sleep, a classic sign of melancholia.']
        ]
    ],
    6 => [
        'title' => 'Development, Course, Cognitive Triad, and Neural Circuitry',
        'slug' => 'development-course-cognitive-triad-neural-circuitry',
        'description' => 'Beck\'s cognitive triad, learned helplessness, monoamine hypothesis, neuroplasticity (BDNF), and HPA axis hyperactivation.',
        'overview' => "The etiology and pathogenesis of Major Depressive Disorder reflect a convergence of cognitive vulnerabilities, neuroendocrine dysregulation, and neurostructural circuit alterations. Rather than representing a simple 'chemical imbalance', contemporary psychiatric neuroscience conceptualizes depression as a failure of neuroplasticity and neural network adaptation within frontostriatal and limbic circuits governing emotional regulation and reward processing.\n\nFrom a psychological perspective, Aaron Beck's cognitive formulation demonstrates that depression is maintained by negative cognitive schemas and the 'Cognitive Triad'—systematically distorted, negative appraisals of: (1) the self ('I am defective/worthless'), (2) the world/ongoing experiences ('The world is hostile/demanding'), and (3) the future ('The future is hopeless'). These schemas fuel automatic negative thoughts through cognitive distortions such as catastrophizing, arbitrary inference, and selective abstraction, reinforcing depressive affect.\n\nNeurobiologically, depression involves hyperreactivity of the amygdala paired with hypoactivity in the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex (DLPFC), impairing top-down cognitive control over emotional distress. Furthermore, chronic stress triggers hyperactivation of the HPA axis, resulting in sustained hypercortisolemia that causes excitotoxic dendritic atrophy in the hippocampus and downregulates brain-derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF), explaining the cognitive slowing and memory deficits seen in chronic depression.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "While cognitive models and neurocircuitry do not form separate DSM-5 diagnostic categories, evaluating these etiological domains is essential for establishing cognitive targets in psychotherapy, assessing chronicity risks, and selecting appropriate pharmacological mechanisms.\n\nClinicians must systematically evaluate: (1) cognitive schema patterns and automatic negative distortions; (2) vegetative signs reflecting neuroendocrine and circadian disruptions; (3) episode duration, cycle acceleration, and inter-episode recovery; and (4) neurocognitive executive functioning deficits across memory, attention, and processing speed.",
        'specifiers' => "Etiological and course specifiers: 'In partial remission', 'In full remission', and dimensional severity grading (Mild, Moderate, Severe).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Depression with Pseudodementia vs. Major Neurocognitive Disorder (Alzheimer's Disease): In depressive pseudodementia, cognitive deficits develop acutely alongside depressed mood, the patient emphasizes their memory failures ('I don't know' on testing), and cognition normalizes with successful antidepressant treatment; in primary dementia, onset is insidious, patients confabulate or minimize deficits, and cognition does not normalize with antidepressants.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 50-year-old executive who presents with severe depression following a corporate reorganization. The executive states: 'I was demoted because I am fundamentally incompetent (negative view of self), everyone in the company looks down on me (negative view of world), and my career is ruined forever (negative view of future).'\n\nNeuropsychological evaluation reveals psychomotor slowing and executive memory difficulties. Morning serum cortisol is markedly elevated. The clinical formulation identifies classical activation of Beck's cognitive triad alongside stress-induced HPA axis dysregulation, establishing the rationale for combined Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT) and neurogenic antidepressant pharmacotherapy.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Beck's Cognitive Triad consists of negative views of the Self, the World (Experiences), and the Future. Learned helplessness (Seligman) demonstrates that uncontrollable stressors induce passive depressive resignation. HPA axis hyperactivity causes hippocampal volume reduction via cortisol neurotoxicity. Depressive pseudodementia features prominent effort-dependent 'I don't know' answers on cognitive testing that resolve with depression remission.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Beck\'s Cognitive Triad', 'icon' => '📐', 'definition' => 'Systematic negative cognitive schemas targeting the Self (worthless), the World (hostile), and the Future (hopeless).'],
            ['title' => 'Learned Helplessness Model', 'icon' => '⛓️', 'definition' => 'Martin Seligman\'s behavioral model where exposure to uncontrollable negative events causes passive depressive paralysis.'],
            ['title' => 'HPA Axis & Cortisol Neurotoxicity', 'icon' => '🧪', 'definition' => 'Chronic stress-induced hypercortisolemia leads to dendritic retraction and volume loss in the hippocampus.'],
            ['title' => 'Depressive Pseudodementia', 'icon' => '🧠', 'definition' => 'Reversible cognitive impairment secondary to severe depression, characterized by \'I don\'t know\' responses on testing.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Cognitive Distortion', 'definition' => 'Systematic errors in reasoning and information processing (e.g., catastrophizing, mind reading) that reinforce negative cognitive schemas.'],
            ['term' => 'Brain-Derived Neurotrophic Factor (BDNF)', 'definition' => 'A critical neurotrophic protein that supports neuronal survival, synaptic plasticity, and neurogenesis, downregulated in chronic depression.'],
            ['term' => 'Pseudodementia', 'definition' => 'A syndrome in which severe depression mimics neurocognitive dementia, distinguished by acute onset and cognitive recovery following mood treatment.'],
            ['term' => 'Frontolimbic Dysregulation', 'definition' => 'An imbalance characterized by hypoactive prefrontal cortical cognitive control over an exaggerated, hyperreactive amygdala.']
        ]
    ],
    7 => [
        'title' => 'Risk & Prognostic Factors, Suicide Lethality, and Demographics',
        'slug' => 'risk-prognostic-factors-suicide-lethality',
        'description' => 'Suicide risk stratification, static vs. dynamic risk factors, lethal means restriction, and demographic prevalence.',
        'overview' => "Major Depressive Disorder is the single psychiatric condition most strongly associated with completed suicide, accounting for more than 50% of all suicide fatalities. Suicide risk assessment is an essential clinical responsibility that requires systematic evaluation of suicidal ideation, intent, planning, access to lethal means, and the balance between static and dynamic risk factors.\n\nA fundamental clinical principle is the distinction between passive death wishes ('I wish I wouldn't wake up in the morning') and active suicidal ideation with plan and intent ('I have stockpiled medication and plan to take it tonight'). While passive ideation is common in depression, the transition to active planning—especially when accompanied by feelings of hopelessness, severe psychic agitation, or preparation behaviors (giving away possessions, writing notes)—represents an immediate psychiatric emergency.\n\nEpidemiologically, MDD exhibits a pronounced gender disparity: starting in early adolescence, females experience roughly twice the rate of unipolar depression compared to males. Regarding suicide, while females have significantly higher rates of suicide attempts, males complete suicide at a rate roughly 3 to 4 times higher, largely due to the selection of higher-lethality methods (such as firearms and hanging).",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Comprehensive Suicide Risk Formulation Parameters:\n\n1. Static Risk Factors (Unmodifiable historical markers): Prior suicide attempt (the single strongest predictor of future completed suicide); family history of suicide; male sex; older age (especially Caucasian males age 65+); history of childhood trauma or abuse.\n\n2. Dynamic Risk Factors (Modifiable clinical variables): Current severe hopelessness; active substance or alcohol intoxication/abuse; acute severe psychic agitation or akathisia; psychotic symptoms (particularly command auditory hallucinations); severe insomnia; acute psychosocial loss (divorce, job loss, financial ruin).\n\n3. Protective Factors: Strong social and family connectedness; responsibility to dependent children or pets; positive therapeutic alliance; active religious beliefs against suicide; future-oriented plans.\n\n4. Lethal Means Assessment: Mandatory inquiry regarding access to firearms, lethal medications, or high places, followed by lethal means restriction protocols.",
        'specifiers' => "Suicide risk formulation must classify current risk level: Low, Moderate, High, or Imminent, directly dictating whether outpatient management, intensive outpatient treatment, or involuntary psychiatric hospitalization is required.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Non-Suicidal Self-Injury (NSSI) vs. Suicidal Behavior: NSSI involves deliberate destruction of body tissue (e.g., cutting, burning) WITHOUT any intent to die, typically used as an maladaptive emotional regulation strategy to relieve intolerable psychic pain; Suicidal behavior involves intent to end one's life.\n\nSuicidal Ideation in MDD vs. Borderline Personality Disorder: Chronic suicidal threats in BPD are often interpersonal communications triggered by abandonment fears, whereas suicidal intent in MDD is driven by pervasive vegetative despair and perceived burdensomeness.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 68-year-old recently widowed male who presents with severe depression and chronic insomnia. The patient states: 'I am a burden to my children and there is no reason to go on.' He admits to writing a will and states he keeps a loaded handgun in his nightstand.\n\nClinical evaluation identifies multiple high-risk static markers (older male, Caucasian, recent loss) and severe dynamic markers (hopelessness, perceived burdensomeness, firearm access, active planning). Because risk of completed suicide is imminent, voluntary or involuntary inpatient psychiatric hospitalization is initiated alongside immediate firearm removal by family members.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: A prior suicide attempt is the single strongest predictor of completed suicide. Men complete suicide 3-4 times more often than women; women attempt suicide 2-3 times more often. Lethal means restriction (especially removing firearms) is the single most effective public health intervention for suicide prevention. Hopelessness is a stronger predictor of suicide than the overall severity of depressive symptoms. Command hallucinations and severe agitation indicate emergency risk.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Prior Attempt as Primary Predictor', 'icon' => '🚨', 'definition' => 'A documented past suicide attempt represents the single most powerful statistical predictor of eventual completed suicide.'],
            ['title' => 'The Gender Paradox of Suicide', 'icon' => '⚖️', 'definition' => 'Females attempt suicide 2-3 times more frequently; males complete suicide 3-4 times more frequently due to high-lethality methods.'],
            ['title' => 'Static vs. Dynamic Risk Stratification', 'icon' => '📊', 'definition' => 'Balancing unmodifiable historical factors (prior attempts, male sex) with treatable clinical factors (hopelessness, agitation, means access).'],
            ['title' => 'Lethal Means Restriction Priority', 'icon' => '🔒', 'definition' => 'Removing access to firearms and toxic medications represents the most proven intervention for reducing immediate suicide mortality.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Lethal Means Restriction', 'definition' => 'The systematic identification and removal of accessible instruments of self-harm (firearms, lethal medication stockpiles) from a patient\'s environment.'],
            ['term' => 'Perceived Burdensomeness', 'definition' => 'A fatal cognitive distortion wherein an individual believes their existence is a liability and that others would be better off if they were dead.'],
            ['term' => 'Akathisia', 'definition' => 'An intense, distressing state of inner motor restlessness, frequently medication-induced, that severely escalates acute suicide risk.'],
            ['term' => 'Non-Suicidal Self-Injury (NSSI)', 'definition' => 'Deliberate, direct destruction of body tissue without suicidal intent, performed to relieve negative affect or induce a positive state.']
        ]
    ],
    8 => [
        'title' => 'Differential Diagnosis: MDD vs. Bereavement vs. Bipolar vs. Adjustment',
        'slug' => 'differential-diagnosis-mdd-bereavement-bipolar-adjustment',
        'description' => 'Elimination of the bereavement exclusion in DSM-5, grief vs. depression distinctions, and adjustment disorder thresholds.',
        'overview' => "Accurate differential diagnosis in depressive presentations requires distinguishing clinical Major Depressive Disorder from normal grief (bereavement), adjustment disorders, and bipolar depression. The clinical distinction carries immense significance: while normal bereavement is a natural, adaptive human response to loss that requires supportive presence rather than pharmacotherapy, major depressive episodes represent severe neurobiological disorders requiring evidence-based clinical intervention.\n\nA major conceptual change in DSM-5 was the complete removal of the historical 'bereavement exclusion'. Under DSM-IV, an individual experiencing depressive symptoms within two months following the death of a loved one was excluded from receiving an MDD diagnosis unless symptoms were exceptionally severe. DSM-5 eliminated this exclusion, recognizing that bereavement does not grant immunity against experiencing a true major depressive episode and that bereavement-induced depression carries identical morbidity, genetics, and suicide risks as depression triggered by other severe stressors.\n\nTo prevent over-pathologizing normal grief, DSM-5 provided explicit clinical guidelines contrasting normal bereavement from major depression: normal grief comes in waves ('pangs of grief') tied to memories of the deceased, self-esteem is preserved, and the person yearns to join the deceased to be with them; in contrast, MDD features pervasive, unremitting despair, pervasive worthlessness, and suicidal thoughts driven by feeling like a burden.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Systematic Differential Checklist:\n\n1. MDD vs. Normal Bereavement: Does the emotional pain come in waves intermixed with positive memories (Grief) or is it pervasive and unremitting (MDD)? Is self-esteem intact (Grief) or are there pervasive feelings of worthlessness and self-hatred (MDD)? Are thoughts of death focused on the deceased (Grief) or driven by worthlessness and inability to cope (MDD)?\n\n2. MDD vs. Adjustment Disorder With Depressed Mood: In Adjustment Disorder, emotional symptoms develop within 3 months of an identifiable stressor, do NOT meet full 5-of-9 criteria for a major depressive episode, and resolve within 6 months of the termination of the stressor or its consequences.\n\n3. MDD vs. Bipolar Depression: Has the patient ever experienced >= 4 days of hypomania or >= 7 days of mania? If yes, the diagnosis is BIPOLAR, never unipolar MDD.",
        'specifiers' => "Diagnostic formulations specify whether depressive symptoms are 'In the context of bereavement' or secondary to specific life transitions.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Persistent Complex Bereavement Disorder (now Prolonged Grief Disorder in DSM-5-TR): Diagnosed when severe yearning, longing, and preoccupation with the deceased persists for at least 12 months in adults (6 months in children), causing marked functional impairment.\n\nMedical Depressive Mimics: Rule out untreated hypothyroidism, systemic lupus, multiple sclerosis, and sleep apnea via objective laboratory testing.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 55-year-old who lost their spouse to cancer five weeks ago. The individual cries when looking at photographs, but between crying episodes can laugh with their grandchildren and feels gratitude for their marriage (mood reactivity, self-esteem preserved). There are no feelings of worthlessness, no psychomotor slowing, and no suicidal ideation.\n\nThe emotional pain comes in waves connected to thoughts of the deceased. Meeting the clinical hallmarks of normal grief with preserved self-esteem and absence of full MDD criteria, the clinical formulation confirms Normal Bereavement, NOT Major Depressive Disorder.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: DSM-5 eliminated the bereavement exclusion; major depression can be diagnosed during bereavement if full 5-of-9 criteria are met. Normal grief features waves of sadness with preserved self-esteem and mood reactivity; MDD features pervasive despair, worthlessness, and anhedonia. Adjustment Disorder does NOT meet full MDD criteria and must resolve within 6 months of stressor termination. Never prescribe antidepressant monotherapy if bipolar history is present.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Elimination of Bereavement Exclusion', 'icon' => '🕊️', 'definition' => 'DSM-5 removed the automatic 2-month bereavement exemption, permitting MDD diagnosis when full clinical criteria are met.'],
            ['title' => 'Waves of Grief vs. Pervasive Despair', 'icon' => '🌊', 'definition' => 'Normal grief manifests in fluctuating pangs tied to memories; major depression involves constant, unremitting negative affect.'],
            ['title' => 'Preserved Self-Esteem in Grief', 'icon' => '🛡️', 'definition' => 'Self-esteem remains intact in normal bereavement, whereas pervasive worthlessness and self-loathing define clinical depression.'],
            ['title' => 'Adjustment Disorder Subthreshold Rule', 'icon' => '📉', 'definition' => 'Adjustment disorder is diagnosed only when symptoms fall short of full 5-symptom MDD criteria following an identifiable stressor.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Bereavement Exclusion', 'definition' => 'The historical DSM-IV diagnostic rule that disqualified individuals from receiving an MDD diagnosis during the initial two months following the death of a loved one.'],
            ['term' => 'Pangs of Grief', 'definition' => 'Paroxysms of acute emotional distress, yearning, and sorrow triggered by reminders of the deceased, characteristic of normal mourning.'],
            ['term' => 'Prolonged Grief Disorder', 'definition' => 'A condition characterized by intense yearning and preoccupation with the deceased persisting beyond 12 months, causing marked functional disability.'],
            ['term' => 'Adjustment Disorder', 'definition' => 'A psychological reaction to an identifiable psychosocial stressor developing within 3 months, causing marked distress but falling short of full MDD criteria.']
        ]
    ],
    9 => [
        'title' => 'Educational Case Review: Clinical Formulations and Symptom Dissection',
        'slug' => 'educational-case-review-depressive-disorders',
        'description' => 'Clinical formulations, symptom timeline tracking, psychometric rating scales, and high-yield board question dissection.',
        'overview' => "Clinical expertise in depressive psychopathology requires synthesizing subjective patient narratives, objective psychometric scales (such as the Patient Health Questionnaire-9 [PHQ-9] and Hamilton Depression Rating Scale [HAM-D]), and longitudinal history. Because patients with depression frequently underreport past hypomanic periods or minimize suicidal planning, the clinician must conduct a rigorous, systematic diagnostic assessment.\n\nFormulating depressive disorders demands precise symptom counting against the 5-of-9 DSM-5 criteria, establishing duration milestones (2 weeks for MDD, 2 years for PDD, 12 months for DMDD), and identifying mandatory clinical specifiers. Furthermore, clinicians must evaluate whether depressive episodes feature mixed manic features, anxious distress, or catatonic motor signs, as these specifiers directly dictate pharmacological choices.\n\nThis educational module analyzes complex case scenarios designed to simulate board examination stems, demonstrating how to systematically apply diagnostic criteria, rule out competing conditions, and justify clinical conclusions.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Systematic Formulation Workflow:\n1. Step 1: Count core criteria (SIGECAPS). Are at least 5 of 9 symptoms present for >= 2 weeks, with either depressed mood or anhedonia? If YES -> Major Depressive Episode.\n2. Step 2: Lifetime Screen for Mood Elevation. Has the patient ever experienced mania or hypomania? If YES -> Bipolar I or II. If NO -> Unipolar Depressive Disorder.\n3. Step 3: Chronicity Evaluation. Has low mood persisted for >= 2 years without > 2 months symptom-free? If YES -> Persistent Depressive Disorder.\n4. Step 4: Apply Specifiers (Melancholic, Atypical, Mixed features, Anxious distress, Peripartum, Seasonal, Psychotic).",
        'specifiers' => "Diagnostic formulations must record primary diagnoses, clinical specifiers, suicide risk stratification, and formal differential exclusions.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Case reviews systematically differentiate: MDD vs. Bipolar II; MDD vs. PDD; MDD vs. Normal Bereavement; and Medication-Induced Depression vs. Primary MDD.",
        'clinical_example' => "Educational Case Vignette: A 42-year-old executive presents with severe low mood, terminal insomnia (waking at 3:30 AM), 15-pound weight loss, and marked psychomotor agitation for four weeks. The executive states that their mood is completely unreactive to good news and is significantly worse every morning.\n\nPsychometric evaluation reveals a PHQ-9 score of 24 (Severe Depression). Lifetime history confirms no prior hypomania or mania. The formulation documents 6 of 9 MDD criteria (depressed mood, insomnia, weight loss, agitation, fatigue, guilt). The non-reactive mood, terminal insomnia, morning worsening, and anorexia satisfy criteria for the 'With melancholic features' specifier, confirming Major Depressive Disorder, Single Episode, Severe, With Melancholic Features.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Formulations require verifying symptom count (5 of 9), duration (2 weeks), and mandatory cardinal symptoms (depressed mood or anhedonia). Always conduct lifetime screening for past hypomania to avoid misdiagnosing Bipolar II. Identify mutually exclusive specifiers (melancholic vs. atypical). Always perform structured suicide risk assessment.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Systematic Diagnostic Workflow', 'icon' => '🪜', 'definition' => 'Structured application of symptom count, duration thresholds, lifetime bipolar screening, and specifier assignment.'],
            ['title' => 'PHQ-9 Psychometric Triangulation', 'icon' => '📊', 'definition' => 'Utilizing validated 9-item clinical rating scales to quantify symptom severity and monitor treatment response over time.'],
            ['title' => 'Cardinal Symptom Requirement', 'icon' => '🎯', 'definition' => 'Verifying that at least one of the qualifying symptoms is either depressed mood or pervasive anhedonia.'],
            ['title' => 'Specifier Phenotypic Mapping', 'icon' => '🗺️', 'definition' => 'Accurately identifying melancholic, atypical, anxious, or mixed specifiers to guide evidence-based somatic treatment.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'PHQ-9', 'definition' => 'Patient Health Questionnaire-9; a widely utilized 9-item self-report screening and diagnostic tool that maps directly onto DSM-5 MDD criteria.'],
            ['term' => 'Hamilton Depression Rating Scale (HAM-D)', 'definition' => 'A clinician-administered depression assessment scale widely used in clinical trials to measure the severity of depressive symptoms.'],
            ['term' => 'Cardinal Symptoms', 'definition' => 'The two primary gateway symptoms of Major Depressive Disorder (depressed mood and anhedonia), at least one of which must be present for diagnosis.'],
            ['term' => 'Syndromal Remission', 'definition' => 'A clinical state wherein an individual no longer meets formal criteria for a major depressive episode and experiences minimal residual symptoms.']
        ]
    ],
    10 => [
        'title' => 'Chapter Mastery & Board-Exam Synthesis',
        'slug' => 'chapter-mastery-board-exam-synthesis-depressive',
        'description' => 'Comprehensive synthesis of Depressive Disorders, duration thresholds, diagnostic hierarchies, and high-yield board pearls.',
        'overview' => "This capstone unit provides a comprehensive synthesis of the Depressive Disorders chapter under DSM-5 criteria, consolidating core diagnostic distinctions, duration thresholds, and high-yield board pearls for professional licensure examinations. Depressive disorders are among the most frequently tested topics across clinical examinations, demanding exact command over diagnostic criteria, specifiers, and differential boundaries.\n\nMastery requires commanding the clinical distinctions separating Major Depressive Disorder, Persistent Depressive Disorder (Dysthymia), Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder (DMDD), and Premenstrual Dysphoric Disorder (PMDD). Furthermore, candidates must navigate DSM-5 structural refinements, including the separation of Depressive Disorders from Bipolar Disorders, the elimination of the bereavement exclusion, the creation of DMDD, and the introduction of the anxious distress specifier.\n\nBy synthesizing clinical vignettes, diagnostic decision matrices, and risk assessment strategies, this unit prepares students and clinicians for board examination excellence and compassionate clinical practice.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Mastery Summary of Core Depressive Diagnostic Rules:\n\n1. Major Depressive Disorder: >= 5 of 9 symptoms for >= 2 weeks, with depressed mood or anhedonia mandatory; lifetime absence of mania/hypomania.\n\n2. Persistent Depressive Disorder: Depressed mood for >= 2 years in adults (>= 1 year in children/adolescents), with >= 2 of 6 symptoms, and no more than 2 consecutive months symptom-free.\n\n3. Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder: Severe temper outbursts >= 3 times/week with chronic inter-outburst irritability, persisting for >= 12 months in >= 2 settings; onset before age 10; diagnosed only between ages 6 and 18; overrides ODD.\n\n4. Premenstrual Dysphoric Disorder: >= 5 symptoms in luteal phase remitting postmenses, confirmed prospectively over >= 2 cycles, with at least 1 affective symptom.\n\n5. Melancholic Features: Non-reactive mood, terminal insomnia, morning worsening, weight loss. Atypical Features: Mood reactivity, hypersomnia, weight gain, leaden paralysis, rejection sensitivity. (Mutually exclusive).",
        'specifiers' => "Review of essential specifiers: Severity grading (Mild, Moderate, Severe), Psychotic features (Mood-congruent vs. Mood-incongruent), Anxious distress, Mixed features, Melancholic features, Atypical features, Peripartum onset, and Seasonal pattern.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Key Board Differential Pearls:\n• Depressive symptoms for 2 weeks -> Major Depressive Disorder.\n• Depressive symptoms for 3 years without >2 months break -> Persistent Depressive Disorder.\n• Child with chronic rages 4x/week and baseline irritability -> DMDD (NOT Bipolar Disorder).\n• Depression in final week before menses remitting at onset -> PMDD (prospective confirmation required).\n• Prior attempt is the #1 predictor of suicide; removing firearms is the #1 lethal means intervention.\n• Bereavement exclusion was eliminated in DSM-5; grief does not prevent an MDD diagnosis.",
        'clinical_example' => "A comprehensive clinical review scenario: A board examination vignette describes a 14-year-old adolescent brought by parents for irritable mood, failing grades, and chronic arguments with teachers over the past five weeks. The adolescent sleeps 10 hours a night, reports feeling constantly exhausted, has gained 10 pounds, and feels rejected by peers. However, when invited to a concert with a close friend, the adolescent smiled and enjoyed the event.\n\nBoard Exam Question Formulation: What is the diagnosis? The candidate recognizes that irritable mood satisfies Criterion A in adolescents, the duration is five weeks (satisfying the 2-week threshold), and mood reactivity alongside hypersomnia, weight gain, and rejection sensitivity satisfies the 'With atypical features' specifier, confirming Major Depressive Disorder, With Atypical Features.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield board pearls summary: MDD = 5 of 9 for 2 weeks with depressed mood or anhedonia. PDD = 2 years with no more than 2 months symptom-free. DMDD = rages >= 3x/week in children age 6-18 with chronic irritability. PMDD = luteal phase 5 symptoms confirmed over 2 cycles. Melancholic and Atypical are mutually exclusive. Prior suicide attempt is the #1 risk factor for suicide. DSM-5 eliminated the bereavement exclusion.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Depressive Diagnostic Decision Matrix', 'icon' => '🏛️', 'definition' => 'Comprehensive command of duration thresholds, symptom counts, and severity rules across all depressive spectrum disorders.'],
            ['title' => 'Pediatric Diagnostic Adjustments', 'icon' => '🧒', 'definition' => 'Irritable mood can substitute for depressed mood in children/adolescents; DMDD captures severe pediatric rages.'],
            ['title' => 'Mutually Exclusive Subtype Axiom', 'icon' => '↔️', 'definition' => 'Melancholic (unreactive mood, terminal insomnia) and Atypical (reactive mood, hypersomnia) can never be co-diagnosed.'],
            ['title' => 'Evidence-Based Suicide Prevention', 'icon' => '🔒', 'definition' => 'Recognizing prior attempt as the primary predictor and lethal means restriction as the primary lifesaving intervention.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'SIGECAPS', 'definition' => 'The standard diagnostic mnemonic for MDD: Sleep, Interest, Guilt, Energy, Concentration, Appetite, Psychomotor, Suicidal ideation.'],
            ['term' => 'Double Depression', 'definition' => 'The clinical phenomenon of a Major Depressive Episode occurring in a patient with pre-existing Persistent Depressive Disorder.'],
            ['term' => 'Mood-Congruent Psychosis', 'definition' => 'Delusions or hallucinations whose content is entirely consistent with depressive themes of personal inadequacy, guilt, death, or punishment.'],
            ['term' => 'Neurogenic Hypothesis', 'definition' => 'The biological theory that therapeutic antidepressant response depends on stimulation of hippocampal neurogenesis and BDNF signaling.']
        ]
    ]
];
